## Global Variable
In Python, a global variable is a variable that is declared outside of all functions and 
is accessible throughout the file (both inside and outside functions).

## Key Concepts
--> Defined at the top level of a script or module
--> Accessible anywhere in that module/file
--> You can read a global variable inside functions without declaring it global
--> To modify a global variable inside a function, you must use the global keyword

    ## Example: Accessing Global Variable
x = 10  # global variable
def show():
    print("Inside function:", x)

show()
print("Outside function:", x)

    ## output:-
Inside function: 10
Outside function: 10

[Note: If you create a variable with the same name inside a function, this variable will be local, 
 and can only be used inside the function. The global variable with the 
 same name will remain as it was, global and with the original value.]

    ## Example
--> Create a variable inside a function, with the same name as the global variable
x = "awesome"
def myfunc():
  x = "fantastic"
  print("Python is " + x)

myfunc()
print("Python is " + x)

## The global Keyword
    Normally, when you create a variable inside a function, that variable is local,
    and can only be used inside that function.
    To create a global variable inside a function, you can use the global keyword.
    
    ## Example
If you use the global keyword, the variable belongs to the global scope:
    
def myfunc():
  global x
  x = "fantastic"

myfunc()

print("Python is " + x)

[Note:- Also, use the global keyword if you want to change a global variable inside
 a function.]

## for Example
To change the value of a global variable inside a function, refer to the variable 
by using the global keyword:

x = "awesome"
def myfunc():
  global x
  x = "fantastic"

myfunc()

print("Python is " + x)
